  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nOur approach involves:\n- Exploiting the structure of the problem by representing the function using piecewise constant compositions with few kinks\n- Using combinatorial optimization to search over discrete parameter spaces\n- Employing constrained optimization to satisfy the integral constraint\n- Balancing continuous and combinatorial search strategies\n- Adapting the number of kinks based on function complexity\n\nThe current method finds local minima in the high-dimensional space. By assuming delocalized structure through few kinks, we can:\n- Reduce dimensionality\n- Better capture known near-optimal configurations from literature\n- Systematically explore configurations that meet current records\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe treat the function as piecewise constant with 0/1 values. The integral constraint is:\n  \u2211h[i] * dx = 1\nWhich gives in discrete terms: \u2211h = n_points/2 = 140 for n_points=280.\n\nKey definitions:\n- Kink positions: Points where function value changes (0<->1)\n- Interval structure: Defined by locations of kinks in [0,2]\n- Integral: Sums of function values proportional to the length of occupied intervals\n\nObjective: Minimize C5 defined as the maximum cross-correlation\n\nConstrained optimization:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u2211h[i] = n_points/2\n- The cross-correlation is computed via discrete correlation\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nParent representation:\n- Start from available initial_h_values (if any) or random valid candidate\n- Function construction with m kinks (m from 1 to 12)\n- Recursive interval partitioning\n\nCombinatorial search:\n- Enumerate valid kink configurations with varying kink counts\n- Use optimal transport to map parent space to candidate space\n- SLSQP for continuous fine-tuning of transition points\n\nObjective computation:\n- Compute cross-correlation using discrete convolution\n- Handle boundary conditions by proper padding\n\nConstraint handling:\n- Constraint projection onto feasible set\n- Iterative repair of boundary violations\n- Rescaling for mass/approximation preservation\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Kink count from 0 to 12 (adaptive)\n- Transition metric choice: Lp-based (p=1,2) with L2 preferred\n- Optimization decomposition:\n  * Coarse grid (combinatorial search)\n  * Fine grid (continuous optimization)\n  * Progressive kink removal from dense to sparse\n\n5. Budget and robustness\n- Primary estimate: 10 seconds for grid search, 90 seconds for optimization\n- Dynamic kink count adaptation maintains robustness\n- Convergence monitoring via residual drop and time\n- Fallback to last validated configuration\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Objective evaluation via valid discrete convolution\n- Candidate repair using projected gradient\n- Feasibility checks:\n  * Sum(h) = 140 +/- \u03b5 (adjustable \u03b5)\n  * Values in [0,1]\n- Memory: Stores best 10 candidates at each level\n- Time: Check timeout during optimization only for fine-tuning\n\nThe computed configuration must satisfy all problem constraints exactly (with clipping within \u03b5).\n\nNote: Implementation implementation assumes n_points=280, dx=2/280=0.007142857142857142",
